                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "A beautiful survival game held back by frustrating gameplay mechanics\n\nRaft has a genuinely enjoyable core concept. Building a floating home from scratch, gradually expanding it, exploring islands, collecting resources and eventually keeping animals aboard is a lot of fun. The game looks lovely, the atmosphere is relaxing, and there's something very satisfying about watching your little collection of floating planks turn into a proper home.\n\nUnfortunately, the longer I play, the more frustrated I become with some of the game's mechanics.\n\nFishing is painfully boring. There's no skill involved, no real variation in fishing locations, and no interesting challenge. You cast, wait, catch a random fish, repeat. And repeat. And repeat. It's not engaging gameplay; it's simply a time-consuming chore. A better fishing rod doesn't fix the underlying problem.\n\nCooking is somehow even worse.\n\nWant to make a soup? First, move your ingredients from your inventory to your hotbar. Then select each ingredient individually using the number keys and place it into the cooking pot. Repeat until all four ingredients are in place.\nNow, after your soup is ready, you can't simply collect it because you have an empty bowl in your inventory. No, the bowl must first be moved to your hotbar and manually selected.\nAnd when you finally collect your soup, does the filled bowl conveniently remain in the same hotbar slot, ready to eat? Of course not! It goes into another slot, so you have to select it again or open your inventory and move it around.\n\nCongratulations! You've successfully completed a small administrative procedure to eat a bowl of soup. 😣\n\nThe recipe system doesn't help either. Ingredients are represented by tiny pictures, and using one wrong fish can turn several valuable ingredients into useless leftovers. A proper cooking interface showing ingredient names and allowing direct selection from the inventory would make an enormous difference.\nInventory management has similar problems. Even checking how much charge remains in a battery requires removing it from the device. Why can't I simply look at the battery and see its charge level?\n\nThe game repeatedly makes me perform unnecessary actions that add no meaningful challenge or enjoyment.\n\nExploration also becomes repetitive. There aren't enough variations of the ordinary islands. After a while, you start recognising the same layouts, collecting the same resources and repeating the same routine. It's a bit like watching the Teletubbies: \"Again! Again!\" And that's frustrating, because I genuinely like so much about this game.\n\nI love building and organising my raft, designing storage areas, growing crops and keeping animals. I enjoy the sense of progression, and the story gives me a reason to keep exploring. There's a really charming game underneath these frustrations. I'm not asking for the survival elements to disappear or for everything to become effortless. I enjoy meaningful resource management, planning and progression. What I don't enjoy is performing the same unnecessary sequence of clicks hundreds of times.\n\nThankfully, the modding community has created solutions for some of these problems. However., other remain frustrating even with mods  and frankly, players shouldn't have to rely on mods to fix basic quality-of-life issues in the first place.\n\nAnd this brings me to why I'm reluctantly giving Raft a negative recommendation.\n\nI genuinely wanted to recommend this game. It has charm, a beautiful world and a wonderful concept. There is a lot here that I enjoy, and I can absolutely understand why other players love it. But when I weigh the positives against the negatives, the frustrating gameplay mechanics unfortunately tip the scales in the wrong direction.\n\nNote: This review is based entirely on my experience playing Raft in single-player mode. I haven't played multiplayer, so I can't comment on how the experience compares.",
